arXiv:2510.04581v1 [cs.CL] 06 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\noautomath</span>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anh Dang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Utrecht University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:t.t.a.dang@uu.nl">t.t.a.dang@uu.nl</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rick Nouwen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Utrecht University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:R.W.F.Nouwen@uu.nl">R.W.F.Nouwen@uu.nl</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Massimo Poesio
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Utrecht University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Queen Mary University of London
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:m.poesio@uu.nl">m.poesio@uu.nl</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to study how LLMs represent and interpret plural reference in ambiguous and unambiguous contexts. We ask the following research questions: (1) Do LLMs exhibit human-like preferences in representing plural reference? (2) Are LLMs able to detect ambiguity in plural anaphoric expressions and identify possible referents? To address these questions, we design a set of experiments, examining pronoun production using next-token prediction tasks, pronoun interpretation, and ambiguity detection using different prompting strategies. We then assess how comparable LLMs are to humans in formulating and interpreting plural reference. We find that LLMs are sometimes aware of possible referents of ambiguous pronouns. However, they do not always follow human reference when choosing between interpretations, especially when the possible interpretation is not explicitly mentioned. In addition, they struggle to identify ambiguity without direct instruction. Our findings also reveal inconsistencies in the results across different types of experiments.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have been shown to reach human-like competence in a wide range of natural language tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite>. Communication between humans and AI systems is becoming more ubiquitous, given their remarkable language understanding and generation capacity. An important factor in effective communication is the ability to handle ambiguity in language. Although humans are very good at resolving ambiguity based on context and common sense knowledge, LLMs are designed with a strong focus on accuracy. In fact, they have been shown to struggle with capturing language ambiguity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wildenburg et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stengel-Eskin et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karamolegkou et al. (2025)</a></cite>. They are often unable to detect or generate ambiguous expressions. Since ambiguity is always present in communication, this inadequacy can lead to misunderstanding and the inability of LLMs to fully comprehend the use of language in context.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04581v1/final1.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/359;" width="476" height="359"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An illustration of the setup and results of our prompting experiments about how the extent to which LLMs can detect ambiguity in anaphoric expressions. Psycholinguistic studies show that in the ambiguous sentence above, humans prefer using <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> to refer to combination of the <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">engine</span> and the <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">boxcar</span> or to one of the objects. We found that LLMs’ responses vary depending on how much information about ambiguity is present in the prompt.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on the ambiguity in coreference resolution. A lot of work has been done on building coreference resolution systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bohnet et al. (2023)</a></cite>. More recent efforts to evaluate LLMs have shown that they can resolve coreference to some extent, yet it is still a challenging task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradhan et al. (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gan et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In actual communication, anaphoric expressions can sometimes be ambiguous. This means that it is not always clear to which referent the anaphoric expression refers. For example, when we say <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Jane looked at Mary and she smiled</span>, the pronoun <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">she</span> possibly refers to Jane or Mary. Without further context, it is impossible to disambiguate the pronoun.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Some research has been conducted on how LLMs process anaphoric expressions. However, most of the work focuses on singular reference and specific cases of ambiguity such as gender-ambiguous pronouns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Webster et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emami et al. (2019)</a></cite>. No research has been done specifically on the processing of plural reference. In this paper, our aim is to study how LLMs conceptualize plural reference in both ambiguous and unambiguous cases. We find plural reference particularly interesting because it is highly dependent on how humans conceptualize plurality. In psycholinguistic research, formulating plural expressions is a complex process because they introduce ambiguity to the discourse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh and Clifton Jr (2002)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2004)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asher and Wang (2003)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patson (2014)</a></cite>. Our goal is to study <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">how LLMs produce, interpret, and represent plural reference in unambiguous and ambiguous contexts</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We design a set of experiments, including next-token prediction and prompting, to compare how comparable LLMs are to humans in producing and interpreting ambiguous and unambiguous plural reference. We ask the following research questions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ1:</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Do LLMs exhibit human-like preferences when representing plural reference?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ2:</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Are LLMs able to detect ambiguity in plural anaphoric expressions and identify possible referents?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our main findings are as follows: (1) LLMs follow human preference when choosing between interpretations in ambiguous cases where the possible referents are directly mentioned and struggle to do so when they are not present in the input; (2) LLMs fail to identify ambiguity without direct instruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a body of work in psycholinguistics that shows that plural reference is more difficult to process and interpret than singular reference because the antecedents of plural anaphoric expressions are usually underspecified <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patson (2014)</a></cite>, leaving more possibilities to interpret them. For example, in the pair of sentences below, the pronoun <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they</span> is more likely to refer to three people in 1(a) than 1(b). In 1(b), <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they</span> may also refer to the group of Tom and Dick.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">.
Ṫom, Dick, and Harry went to a restaurant last night. They ate a pizza.
.̱ Tom and Dick went to a restaurant with Harry last night. They ate a pizza.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we focus specifically on the cases where there is an interaction between singular and plural reference. These are cases where singular and plural expressions are both probable. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite> find that the degree of overlap in roles between objects affects whether they are grouped as a plural entity. For example, in
sentences 2(a) and 2(b), <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">waitress</span> is more related to <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">waiter</span> than <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">customer</span> in that they are more likely to perform the same action. As such, <span id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they</span> is more likely to be the continuation of 2(a) than 2(b). 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para"><span id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">.
Ṫhe waiter and the waitress were in the restaurant. __ (<span id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">He/She/They</span>)
.̱ The waiter and the customer were in the restaurant. __ (<span id="S2.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">He/She/They</span>)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a></cite> investigate how humans interpret singular and plural pronouns in
contexts where there is a whole-part relation between entities.
When two singular objects are introduced to the discourse, and they are combined into a unified object, the new unified object is known as a <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mereological</span> object <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poesio et al. (2006)</a></cite>. It has been found that when more than one object is present, a plural expression is preferred and the use of a singular expression can cause processing difficulty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2004)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford and Moxey (1995)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gordon et al. (1999)</a></cite>. However, there are cases where a singular reference may become salient as a new entity is created by combining the two antecedents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para"><span id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">.
Ṫhe engineer attached the <span id="S2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">engine</span> to the <span id="S2.p6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">boxcar</span> and sent __ (<span id="S2.p6.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">it/them</span>)
.̱ The engineer detached the <span id="S2.p6.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">engine</span> from the <span id="S2.p6.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">boxcar</span> and sent __ (<span id="S2.p6.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">it/them</span>)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the two sentences above. In 3(a), through the verb <span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attached</span>, the engine and the boxcar are combined, introducing a new singular object to the discourse. This new object is a <span id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">train</span>, though it is never explicitly mentioned. Now, a singular or plural reading is both appropriate. The pronoun following the verb <span id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sent</span> can be <span id="S2.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> or <span id="S2.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them</span>, with <span id="S2.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> referring to one of the parts (that is, the engine or the boxcar) or the new object (that is, the train). In 3(b), however, the verb <span id="S2.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">detached</span> prevents the formation of the mereological entity, as such, when <span id="S2.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> is used, it can only refer to one of the objects.
<span id="S2.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Them</span> is used to refer to the group of two objects. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a></cite> found that humans prefer using <span id="S2.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> more in 3(a) than 3(b). Moreover, they are more likely to consider the mereological object rather than the constituents to be the referent of <span id="S2.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. This suggests that when entities are joined, a new unified <span id="S2.p7.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> group</span> entity is created, making <span id="S2.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> a preferred antecedent for singular pronouns. In the following sections, we study how LLMs produce and interpret pronouns in these ambiguous cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Ambiguity in LLMs</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ambiguity is
when an expression can be interpreted in different ways in a context.
The use of everyday language is full of ambiguity, and it is shown that ambiguity indeed facilitates effective communication by minimizing effort <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Piantadosi et al. (2012)</a></cite>. With LLMs, recognizing ambiguity can be challenging, as they are not trained for this purpose and do not have access to pragmatic contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An emerging body of research has been done on how LLMs handle ambiguity in input. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stengel-Eskin et al. (2023)</a></cite> focus on semantic parsing. They prompt LLMs with ambiguous sentences with two possible interpretations and ask them to respond with the logical parses that correspond to the interpretations. They found that LLMs are unable to capture the possible interpretations in zero-shot contexts but perform much better in the few-shot contexts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sedova et al. (2024)</a></cite> look at entity ambiguity. They show that while LLMs are aware of the possible readings, they often struggle with explicitly verbalizing the ambiguity. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite> use a prompting-based approach to test the ability of LLMs in disambiguating, recognizing, and modeling different types of linguistic ambiguity. They found that even <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4</span> still struggles with capturing ambiguity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the domain of coreference resolution, some studies have considered coreference to be one of the main sources of ambiguity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wildenburg et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamath et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gan et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, the types of ambiguity they cover are quite limited <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wildenburg et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamath et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite> or strongly focus on unambiguous reference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gan et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The instances related to coreference account for a small number in their datasets. As such, in this work, we aim for a more extensive investigation of different types of plural reference ambiguity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two types of experiments to study how LLMs process ambiguous plural references. For both experiments, we test a number of recent autoregressive decoder-only LLMs, namely <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a></cite>, <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dang et al. (2024)</a></cite> and <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite>. We evaluate the ability of LLMs to process ambiguous plural anaphoric expressions along the following dimensions. These capabilities are tested using a combination of prompting and next-token prediction tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Production</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">is the ability to predict the correct pronoun for the referents. We test whether LLMs are aware of the pronouns for all possible antecedents. For example, in mereological contexts, are they able to identify that both singular (mereological object) and plural pronouns (constituents) are appropriate?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">means being able to directly specify the possible antecedents of the pronoun. Several prompting experiments will be used to evaluate this ability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Detection</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">refers to the ability to identify whether the pronoun can have multiple possible antecedents. This ability can be demonstrated through the extent to which LLMs can provide meta-linguistic judgment of the ambiguity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Production</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Simple Plural References</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first look at the case of simple plural reference as a baseline experiment, where the referents of the pronouns are completely unambiguous. When the direct object is singular, the pronoun referring to that object must also be singular. In the experiment, we asked the LLMs to predict the pronoun for both singular and plural referents.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> to generate the dataset <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              The GitHub repository consisting of the datasets and code is available at <a href="https://github.com/dangthithaoanh/ambiguous-plural-reference" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/dangthithaoanh/ambiguous-plural-reference</a> </span></span></span>. It consisted of 74 pairs of sentence prefixes. After the generation, we manually inspect each sentence pair to ensure that they meet the following requirements: (1) The direct object must be a countable object and (2) the second verb must be plausible with both the singular and plural versions of the direct object. Each pair differs in the number of the direct object, causing a difference in the choice of pronoun following the second verb. For each sentence prefix, there is only one correct pronoun. See the pair of sentences below for an example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">.
Ṫhe Michelin chef prepared the dish and served __
.̱ The Michelin chef prepared the dishes and served __</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table  <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Results ‣ 5.1 Simple Plural References ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the mean probability of singular and plural pronouns and the difference between them. As expected, we found that LLMs produce much higher probabilities for the correct pronouns for the sentence prefixes. This shows that, in simple unambiguous contexts, LLMs are fully capable of producing correct pronouns for the antecedents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
