</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Split Antecedents Reference</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite> find that the degree of overlap between roles assigned to characters predicts the formation of a plural reference object. If LLMs exhibit a preference similar to that of humans, we would expect that the probabilities of the plural pronoun in 2(a) are higher than in 2(b), as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite>. Following the study, we include another condition in which the two characters are linked by the conjunction <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with</span> instead of <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span>. There are studies showing that the conjunction <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with</span> may decrease the preference for using plural pronouns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albrecht and Clifton (1998)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford and Lockhart (1990)</a></cite>. The pair of sentences below shows the difference between the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with</span> conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">.
Ṫhe waiter and the waitress were in the restaurant. __
.̱ The waiter was in the restaurant with the waitress. __</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset is adopted from the materials in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite>. Each condition consists of 24 sentence prefixes. The conditions include one with a high overlap between roles or spatial status. In addition to the overlap of the roles, there are also two other conditions where the conjunction linking the two roles is different, namely <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with</span>. Using a <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">2x2</span> setting, this results in 4 conditions per example. See Table  <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Results ‣ 5.3.2 Experiment 2: Object Production ‣ 5.3 Mereological Reference ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for an example of all conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metric</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each prefix, we extract and compare the probability difference between singular and plural pronouns. Possible singular pronouns include <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">he</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">she</span> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{he/she}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{he/she}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We
consider the pronoun with
the highest probability.
Plural pronoun is <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they</span> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{they}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{they}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
