<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Results of Probability Prediction Task for Split-antecedent Plural References. The <span id="S5.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">High</span> and <span id="S5.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Low</span> conditions represent high or low overlap between roles. <span id="S5.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">And</span> and <span id="S5.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With</span> are the conjunction linking the two roles. <span id="S5.T2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Diff.</span> is the probability difference between the singular and plural pronoun (<math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{sing}-P_{plur}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{sing}-P_{plur}</annotation></semantics></math>), averaged across examples.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the probabilities of singular and plural continuations between conditions. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Metric ‣ 5.2 Split Antecedents Reference ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the mean probabilities between the targeted pronouns in all conditions for both LLMs. We run a two-way ANOVA with <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">role overlap</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">conjunction</span> as independent variables. The dependent variable is the probability difference between the singular and plural pronouns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We find that the main effect of <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">role overlap</span> was significant (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 35.04, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt;.001; <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 16.7, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt;.001; <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 17.96, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt;.001). The main effect of <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">conjunction</span> was also not significant for <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 1.23, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .277) but was significant for <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 5.85, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .023) and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 33.37, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt; .001). The interaction effect between <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">conjunction</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">role overlap</span> was not significant (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 1.23, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .277; <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = .44, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .512; <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.30" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F</span>(1, 23) = 1.54, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .226). This result is consistent with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite>, suggesting that the degree of overlap of the roles between the antecedents affects how LLMs assign probabilities to singular and plural pronouns.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Mereological Reference</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report experiments on the production of singular and plural pronouns in mereological contexts, a type of ambiguous plural reference where two distinct objects
also form a unified object.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset of this task consists of 44 pairs of mereological and non-mereological sentences. Half of the dataset is adapted from the material from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a></cite>. We manually generate more pairs of sentences following the same template. Before running the main experiment, we want to ensure that the model is aware of the mereological object formed by attaching the two objects. We annotate the dataset with the names of the mereological objects and create another version of the dataset, in which the original sentence prefixes are appended with the token <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span>. Each sentence in the dataset for this experiment thus has the following template:
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{The engineer attached the engine to the goods wagon and sent the; engine; wagon; train}</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then have the LLMs predict the next token of the mereological sentences, where the mereological objects may have a higher chance of being observed. We then look at the top 15 tokens with the highest probabilities. If the mereological object is one of those tokens, then the sentence prefix is qualified. Otherwise, we remove the prefix from the dataset. This results in a total of 44 pairs of sentences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P_{it}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒕</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P_{it}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P_{them}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mrow><mi>𝒕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P_{them}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff.</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">.336</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">.009</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">.326</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Non-mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.484</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.014</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.470</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.352</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.009</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.343</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Non-mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.467</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.012</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.455</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.363</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.005</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">.357</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">Non-mereo.</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">.507</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">.012</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">.494</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Results of Probability Prediction Task for Mereological Reference. The <span id="S5.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Diff.</span> column represents the mean probability difference between <span id="S5.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> and <span id="S5.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them (<math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{it}-P_{them}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{it}-P_{them}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1 </span>Experiment 1: Pronoun Production</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we test whether LLMs prefer singular pronouns in mereological contexts when the new objects are created. Through a next-word prediction task, we extract the probabilities of <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them</span> in the mereological and non-mereological sentences. If the pronoun is <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>, it refers to the individual object (that is, engine or boxcar) or the mereological object (i.e., train).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metric</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the probability difference between <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them</span> in the mereological (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}</annotation></semantics></math>) and non–mereological (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math>) sentences. The following formulas show how we calculate the difference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.1" class="ltx_logical-block">
            
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo}=P_{it|mereo}-P_{them|mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}=P_{it|mereo}-P_{them|mereo}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
            
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N.mereo}=P_{it|N.mereo}-P_{them|N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}=P_{it|N.mereo}-P_{them|N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
          
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the difference is larger in the mereological sentences than the non-mereological sentences, then the LLMs show a higher preference for <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>, as found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table  <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Dataset ‣ 5.3 Mereological Reference ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrates how LLMs assign probabilities to <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them</span> in mereological and non-mereological cases. It can be seen that LLMs prefer using <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> in both contexts. We run paired-sample <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span>-tests, comparing <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math> across all pairs of sentences. The results show that the difference is significantly larger in non-mereological contexts (<span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -4.78, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt; .001; <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -3.88, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt; .001; <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -5.73, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt;.001). The statistical results show the opposite of what we expected. LLMs give higher probability to the singular pronoun when processing non-mereological sentences. It should be noted that this result does not reflect whether LLMs give a higher preference to the mereological object in mereological contexts because we do not yet know what <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> refers to. As such, we run another experiment, in which we consider the probabilities of the objects themselves.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2 </span>Experiment 2: Object Production</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the first experiment, we observe that <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> has very high probabilities in both contexts. However, while the pronoun <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">them</span> clearly refers to the group of two objects, the antecedent of the pronoun <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> can be highly ambiguous. It can refer to either the mereological object or one of the constituents. We also observe that the probability of
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> is quite high:
in most cases, the next most probable token is <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span>, which can be followed by the individual object or the mereological object.
Thus, evidence for mereological object preference can also be demonstrated when the direct object of the verb is specified. As such, we conducted another experiment with <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> after the original prefix. Adding <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> forces the LLMs to specify the object to be referenced.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same dataset from Experiment 1 and append <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> to the sentence prefixes. See Table  <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Results ‣ 5.3.2 Experiment 2: Object Production ‣ 5.3 Mereological Reference ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for a complete example of the dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metric</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the probabilities of the individual objects with higher probabilities (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ind.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ind.}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the mereological object (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{mereo.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{mereo.}</annotation></semantics></math>). See the formulas below for the detailed calculation of the metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.1" class="ltx_logical-block">
            
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{mereo}=P_{ind.|mereo}-P_{mereo.|mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}=P_{ind.|mereo}-P_{mereo.|mereo}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
            
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{N.mereo}=P_{ind.|N.mereo}-P_{mereo.|N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}=P_{ind.|N.mereo}-P_{mereo.|N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
          
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the probability difference between the mereological objects and the constituents in the mereological cases (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}</annotation></semantics></math>) is higher than that in the non-mereological cases (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math>), then the LLMs show a greater preference to choose the mereological objects as the referent for the pronoun <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> in mereological contexts.</p>
</div>
