</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table  <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Metric ‣ 5.3.2 Experiment 2: Object Production ‣ 5.3 Mereological Reference ‣ 5 Production ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we display the mean probability of individual objects, the mereological objects, as well as the difference between them. For <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>, the mean probability of the mereological object is higher than its constituents. Paired-sample <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span>-tests show that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo}</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly larger than <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -2.845, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .006; <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -2.669, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> = .01 ; <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> = -5.825 , <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span> &lt; .001). This result suggests that when directly choosing an object for the second verb (e.g. <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sent</span>), the LLMs do prefer the joined object more in the mereological contexts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
