<td id="S5.T5.2.1.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FF85FF;">mereo. obj.</span>/ <span id="S5.T5.2.1.10.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FF85FF;">ind obj</span>.</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{mereo.}-D_{N.mereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{mereo.}-D_{N.mereo}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.2.1.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b"></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">The engineer detached the engine from the goods wagon and sent the __</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b"></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Examples of the datasets used in all experiments. The types with expected pronouns in <span id="S5.T5.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#61FFE0;">blue</span> mean that there can only be one possible pronoun and it is unambiguous. Those in <span id="S5.T5.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FF85FF;">pink</span> mean that multiple pronouns are possible.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Interpretation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Section 5 aims at testing whether LLMs are able to produce correct pronouns in unambiguous and ambiguous contexts, in this Section we look at how LLMs interpret these pronouns. Do they interpret them differently in ambiguous contexts? Do they notice possible ambiguity? To do this, we use various prompting techniques to study the extent to which LLMs can detect ambiguity and generate possible referents for the pronouns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Previous studies have found that while LLMs may be aware of the potential ambiguity in input, they are sometimes unable to transform this knowledge into a textual explanation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stengel-Eskin et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karamolegkou et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Taking into account such a possibility, we use two prompting directions. In Prompt 1 (<span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1</span>), we ask LLMs to
provide all possible referents for a pronoun.
In Prompt 2 (<span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span>),
LLMs have to choose the referent for the pronoun among those in a supplied list.
Some of the listed referents suggest ambiguity,
to see to what extent the LLMs can make use of them to detect ambiguity.
In Prompt 3 (<span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span>), we ask LLMs if the mereological object can be the referent.
Finally, we ask if the LLMs think that the pronoun <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> is ambiguous (<span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span>).
All of our prompts are few-shot.
See Appendix  <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt Construction ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for details about our prompt designs and how we code LLMs’ responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For baseline comparison, we also prompt LLMs on the simple reference dataset. The experiments are run on two LLMs, namely <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> and <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Reference Identification</h3>

<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unambiguous Reference</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a baseline, we first test whether LLMs can choose the correct antecedent for simple unambiguous reference using the dataset described in Section 5.1. The results are not surprising. The LLMs reach 100% accuracy. This shows that in unambiguous contexts, LLMs are fully able to choose the correct antecedents for pronouns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ambiguous Reference</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the results of <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span> for the mereological dataset. When being asked to list all possible referents for <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>, the LLMs show somewhat different patterns. <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> consistently chooses one of the constituents as the only referent in both mereological and non-mereological contexts. <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> is better at recognizing that the pronoun is ambiguous and that both objects can be the referent (50%). However, both rarely mention the mereological object as a possible referent (11.3%).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure  <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.2 Ambiguity Detection ‣ 6 Interpretation ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (top, left) shows the percentage of each answer choice in mereological and non-mereological contexts. When LLMs are forced to choose an antecedent for
pronoun <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>, they generally prefer to link
<span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> to the first object. Notably, for <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>, the number of times the LLMs choose the first object is much higher
in the non-mereological cases than in the mereological cases. However, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> gives nearly equal preference for the first object and the mereological object. The preference for the second object is quite similar in both cases. As a result, the percentage of choosing the mereological object is higher in the mereological cases (<span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: 37.3%, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: 22%) than the non-mereological cases (22.8%). This shows that LLMs give higher preference to the mereological object in mereological contexts. However, they seem to be more likely to link the pronoun <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> to the constituent in both cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another answer choice that we are also interested in is <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D. any of the above objects</span>. If the LLMs choose <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span>, they think that all objects are equally likely to be the referent for the pronoun <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. In fact, if the models understand that <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> is ambiguous, this option has to be the most-chosen one. The low percentage of this option shows that, when being asked, the LLMs tend to specify an object as a referent instead of accepting the ambiguity. This shows that when the LLMs are instructed towards choosing a single referent, they often fail to identify the ambiguity that is present.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A surprising result is that <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> chooses <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> as referring to the mereological object quite often in the non-mereological prompts (21.3%), where the mereological object is not available as a possible antecedent.
This can be
an effect of word frequency, such that the mereological object is more frequent or more likely to be preceded by the main verb.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Ambiguity Detection</h3>

<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04581v1/mainplot.png" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/276;" width="476" height="276" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Results of prompting experiments with four different prompts (P1, P2, P3, and P4). The <span id="S6.F2.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">x</span>-axis displays different types of LLMs’ responses. In <span id="S6.F2.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1</span>, <span id="S6.F2.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ind.</span> is the one of the constituent. <span id="S6.F2.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ind. + Mereo.</span> means that they listed both the constituents and the mereological object. In <span id="S6.F2.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span>, the LLMs choose between the constituents (<span id="S6.F2.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Obj.1</span> and <span id="S6.F2.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Obj. 2</span>, the mereological object (<span id="S6.F2.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mereo. Obj.</span> or any of the mentioned objects (<span id="S6.F2.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Any</span>). In <span id="S6.F2.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span>, they answered <span id="S6.F2.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="S6.F2.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span> to whether the mereological object can be the referent. In <span id="S6.F2.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span>, the LLMs all identified <span id="S6.F2.30" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> as ambiguous. The <span id="S6.F2.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ambiguous + Mereo.</span> column shows when the LLMs mention the mereological object as a possible referent. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now examine the results of <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span> where we test the extent to which LLMs can detect ambiguity given direct mention. Recall that in <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span>, we ask the LLMs if <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> is ambiguous. We also run this experiment on unambiguous sentences. We find that in 97.7% of the cases, they correctly identify the pronoun as unambiguous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure  <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.2 Ambiguity Detection ‣ 6 Interpretation ‣ Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (bottom, right) shows the percentage of <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Yes</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">No</span> responses. We found that LLMs consistently identify the pronoun <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> as an ambiguous one (100%) in both mereological and non-mereological
cases. This is rather surprising given that in the <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span> they almost never choose
the option that indicates ambiguity (i.e. any of the
above objects). In addition, in the explanations of
LLMs, they also generate possible antecedents of <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. In
13.6% (<span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>) and 18.2% (<span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>) of the mereological prompts, they include the mereological object as a possible antecedent,
compared to 2.3% in the non-mereological prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span>, we ask the LLMs if the mereological object
can be the referent of <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. The percentage of <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Yes</span> in the mereological cases is moderate for both LLMs (<span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>: 61.4%, <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>: 59.1%).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="150.48" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 150.48" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,150.48) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 144.57 C 0 147.84 2.64 150.48 5.91 150.48 L 647.24 150.48 C 650.51 150.48 653.15 147.84 653.15 144.57 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 129.21 L 651.18 129.21 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 135.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">SAMPLE RESPONSE - AYA-EXPANSE-8B</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:7.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="103.62" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 103.62)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the referent of the pronoun ’it’ in the following sentence: 
<br class="ltx_break">The hardworking, experienced autoworker removed the suspension from the chassis and sent it __. 
<br class="ltx_break">Can ’it’ refer to the car? 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>
<span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt; Yes, in the given sentence, the pronoun ’it’ can refer to the car. The sentence is discussing an action performed on a car, specifically the removal of the suspension from the chassis. Since ’it’ is used to refer to the object that is being acted upon, it logically follows that ’it’ refers to the car.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, these percentages are much higher than those in <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span>. Yet, on the other hand, the difference between mereological and non-mereological contexts is quite small. Especially for <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span>, the results are similar in the non-mereological cases, which is counterintuitive because the mereological object should not be available as a referent given the detaching action. The text above is an example response where <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B</span> thinks that <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> can refer to the mereological object in the non-mereological sentence. When the <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">car</span> (mereological object) is mentioned in the prompt, the model believes that the suspension and the chassis are parts of a car. When the autoworker removes one of the parts (e.g., the suspension), the remaining is still a car. However, this line of reasoning does not appear in <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span>, when the mereological object is not mentioned in the non-mereological prompt. The model simply responds that both constituents can be the referent. This suggests a crucial difference in LLMs’ preference when a potential referent is mentioned in the prompt or not.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the above experiments, we assess LLMs’ ability to identify different types of coreference ambiguity using probability-based and prompting-based approaches. In the split-antecedent references, LLMs show a strong alignment with human preference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2004)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite>. When the roles assigned to the antecedents indicate that they may be involved in the same action, they are more likely to be referenced as a plural entity. However, in mereological contexts, the results are more complex. On the one hand, LLMs show preference for the mereological object following the verb, as shown in the <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Object Production</span> experiment. On the other hand, the <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Interpretation</span> experiment shows the lack of self-consistency of LLMs between prompting strategies and hints about ambiguity and the mereological objects. Without hints of ambiguity, they almost never recognize the ambiguity when being prompted with <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1</span> and <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span>, although these prompts do not exclude the possibility that the pronoun is ambiguous. They also do not explicitly mention the mereological object as a referent except when it is provided in the prompt. Generally, LLMs prefer to link <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> to the first constituent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, in <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2</span> and <span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span>, where the mereological objects are presented in the prompts, they are chosen or listed much more frequently (40%–60%). This leads to the possibility that LLMs may exhibit this psycholinguistic phenomenon when direct instruction is provided. This hypothesis is complemented by the results of the <span id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Object Production</span> experiment where some LLMs (<span id="S7.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aya-Expanse-8B and Qwen2.5-7B</span>) give higher probabilities to mereological objects in mereological contexts. This possibility aligns with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stengel-Eskin et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karamolegkou et al. (2025)</a></cite>, who find that LLMs are better at producing different interpretations in few-shot contexts. Taken together, these results may imply that LLMs may not be able to apply their knowledge of ambiguity without specific instruction. In other words, they sometimes know the possible interpretations, but struggle to consistently show them in different types of instruction. This may be the case, as LLMs successfully detect ambiguity between the constituents in <span id="S7.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4</span>. However, the results of <span id="S7.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3</span>, where the difference between mereological and non-mereological contexts is not considerable, the mention of the mereological objects may not mean that the LLMs actually recognize the joined object. As mentioned before, the effect of frequency may be present. This suggests that the LLMs may be able to detect potential referents when they are mentioned in the input but fail to take into account the mereological object, a more complex referent. This suggests that while LLMs broadly capture the basic findings about plural pronoun preferential interpretation from classic papers such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2004)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moxey et al. (2012)</a></cite>, their preferences differ from those captured in more recent literature such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cokal et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also observe that, while the LLMs perform quite similarly in the next-word prediction task, they exhibit different patterns in prompting tasks. <span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> seems to align more with humans. The model indeed chooses the mereological objects more in mereological contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we conduct a set of experiments to investigate whether LLMs are able to capture different interpretations of ambiguous plural reference and whether their representations of reference are comparable to how humans do in psycholinguistic literature. Our mixed findings contribute to a larger picture of the degree to which LLMs possess knowledge of ambiguity and how this knowledge is used.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study provides new insights into how LLMs process text with different types of coreference ambiguity. Yet it has a few limitations that need to be noted. First, in the mereological cases, we think that the frequency of the constituents and the mereological object affect its likelihood to be chosen by the LLMs in both the next-token prediction and prompt tasks. For example, in the sentence <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The shoemaker stripped the leather from the sole and inspected it __</span>, the mereological object <span id="S9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shoe</span> is much more common than the <span id="S9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">leather</span> or the <span id="S9.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sole</span>. Although we try to select the verb that can be done on both constituents and the mereological objects, this effect of frequency is likely to be present, leading the LLMs to choose the mereological object as the referent without knowledge of the mereological relation. Secondly, the LLMs that we tested are rather small (around 7-8B). Investigations into larger LLMs are important as they may exhibit higher competency in interpreting ambiguous pronouns. In addition, we rely on prompting results, which may not always reflect the representation and reasoning capability of the LLMs. Future research may use recent mechanistic interpretability approaches for a more thorough and dimension-wise investigation of how LLMs represent ambiguous references <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Syed et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vig et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanna and Mueller (2025)</a></cite>. Such methods involve interventions on the LLMs’ hidden activations, which potentially uncover the process underneath the LLMs’ generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, as pointed out by the reviewers, we may be quite strict in evaluating LLMs’ ability to provide metalinguistic judgements about ambiguity because even humans can still have difficulty identifying it. We did not conduct the prompting experiments on humans, so we do not know their preferences. We acknowledge this as one of the limitations of the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study uses only publicly available linguistic stimuli from previous research and synthetic data generated by LLMs. It does not include any data from human participants.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank three anonymous reviewers for their insightful comments. This project is funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO) through the AiNed Fellowship Grant (Dealing with Meaning Variation, NGF.1607.22.002) to Massimo Poesio.</p>
</div>
</section>
