</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib32" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Yu et al. (2020)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Juntao Yu, Nafise Sadat Moosavi, Silviu Paun, and Massimo Poesio. 2020.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Free the plural: Unrestricted split-antecedent anaphora resolution.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib32.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proceedings of the 28th International Conference on Computational Linguistics</em>, pages 6113–6125.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Prompt Construction</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Prompt without Explicit Hints of Ambiguity</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.1 </span>Prompt 1: Open-ended Questions</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the first prompt, we ask the LLMs to generate all possible antecedents for <span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. The goal is to see whether they have a deterministic choice or also consider other possible candidates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="103.52" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 103.52" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,103.52) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 97.61 C 0 100.87 2.64 103.52 5.91 103.52 L 647.24 103.52 C 650.51 103.52 653.15 100.87 653.15 97.61 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 79.41 L 651.18 79.41 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 88)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Prompt template 1</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 14.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:3.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.04em;font-size:10pt;" height="53.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 53.28)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the pronoun "it" in the following sentence prefix. What is the referent of "it"? List all possible referents if there are any. 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: &lt;sentence&gt;<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt;<span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">See the excerpt below for an example prompt and the LLM’s response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the referent of the pronoun ’it’ in the following sentence: 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: The bike rider clipped the wheel to the frame and checked it __ 
<br class="ltx_break">What is the referent of ’it’? List all possible referents if any.</p>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_p">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt; In the sentence "The bike rider clipped the wheel to the frame and checked it," the pronoun "it" likely refers to either "the wheel" or "the frame." 
<br class="ltx_break">Given the sequence of actions described, the most logical interpretation would be that the bike rider has just clipped the wheel to the frame and then proceeded to check the frame for proper attachment or alignment. However, without additional context, there’s also a possibility that "it" could refer to the wheel again, checking to ensure that the wheel has been securely attached to the frame. 
<br class="ltx_break">Therefore, the possible referents for "it" are: 
<br class="ltx_break">1. The wheel 
<br class="ltx_break">2. The frame 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We divide LLMs’ responses into two categories. If the LLMs only list one of the constituents, their responses are coded as <span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ind.</span>. If they choose between the two constituents, these are coded as <span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ind. + Ind.</span> In case they mention both constituents and the mereological object, responses are coded as <span id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ind. + Mereo.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.2 </span>Prompt 2: Multiple-choice Questions</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We append the expected pronoun to the sentence prefixes and ask the model to choose from the three objects using a multiple-choice format. To control for the effect of prompt difference, we use two types of prompts. In the multiple-choice question, there are two other options (<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D. any of the above objects</span>), which imply that the pronoun is ambiguous and any of the mentioned objects can be the pronoun’s referent and (<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">E. none of the above object</span>). Previous studies on how LLMs respond to multiple-choice questions have shown that LLMs prefer certain answer symbols, regardless of the actual content. In order to control for this effect, we shuffle the order of the options except for option D and E, resulting in a total of three different runs, and take the mean over runs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="209.37" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 209.37" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,209.37) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 203.46 C 0 206.72 2.64 209.37 5.91 209.37 L 647.24 209.37 C 650.51 209.37 653.15 206.72 653.15 203.46 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 185.26 L 651.18 185.26 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 193.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Prompt template 2</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 163.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:10.84em;font-size:10pt;" height="159.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the pronoun "it" in the following sentence: 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: &lt;sentence&gt; 
<br class="ltx_break">Which object does "it" refer to? 
<br class="ltx_break">A. first object 
<br class="ltx_break">B. second object 
<br class="ltx_break">C. mereological object 
<br class="ltx_break">D. any of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break">E. none of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break">Answer: 
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt;<span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">

</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">See the excerpt below for an example prompt
and the LLM’s response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the pronoun "it" in the following sentence: 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: John put the card into the envelope and hid it __. 
<br class="ltx_break">Which object does "it" refer to? 
<br class="ltx_break">A. the card 
<br class="ltx_break">B. the envelope 
<br class="ltx_break">C. the letter 
<br class="ltx_break">D. any of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break">E. none of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break">Answer: 
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt; The answer is A. the card. 
<br class="ltx_break">In the sentence "John put the card into the envelope and hid it," the pronoun ’it’ refers to the last object mentioned before it, which is ’the card.’ This is a common usage of pronouns in English, where ’it’ is often used to refer back to the nearest noun in a sentence.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The answer choices are coded as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">– <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Obj. 1</span>: The first object 
<br class="ltx_break">– <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Obj. 2</span>: The second object 
<br class="ltx_break">– <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mereo.</span>: The mereological object 
<br class="ltx_break">– <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Any.</span>: Any of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break">– <span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">None.</span>: None of the above objects 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Prompt with Explicit Mention of Ambiguity</h3>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.1 </span>Prompt 3: Open-ended Questions</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We add various hints of ambiguity to study whether LLMs’ responses are affected. To see if the LLMs are aware of the mereological object. We directly ask the LLMs if the mereological objects can be the referent for <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. Responses are coded as either <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="120.12" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 120.12" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,120.12) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 114.22 C 0 117.48 2.64 120.12 5.91 120.12 L 647.24 120.12 C 650.51 120.12 653.15 117.48 653.15 114.22 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 96.01 L 651.18 96.01 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 104.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Prompt template 3</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 14.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:5.05em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.04em;font-size:10pt;" height="70.42" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69.88)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the pronoun "it" in the following sentence. Can "it" refer to the &lt;mereological object&gt;? Provide the answer and explain. 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: &lt;sentence&gt;<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt;<span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">See the excerpt below for an example prompt
and the LLM’s response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p">&lt;USER&gt; Consider the referent of the pronoun ’it’ in the following sentence: 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: The shoemaker glued the leather to the sole and inspected it __. 
<br class="ltx_break">Can ’it’ refer to the shoe? 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_p">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt;
Yes, "it" can refer to the shoe in this sentence. The context suggests that after the shoemaker has glued the leather to the sole, they then inspect the completed shoe or the work as a whole. Without additional context, the most natural and probable interpretation is that "it" refers to the shoe that has been recently made. However, depending on the broader context of the paragraph or the surrounding sentences, there could potentially be other referents for "it."</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.2 </span>Prompt 4: Open-ended Questions</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we use an open question format to test whether LLMs capture the fact that the pronoun <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> can refer to multiple referents. As shown in <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">prompt 2</span>, the model was asked to tell whether the pronoun <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> is ambiguous and explain their answer. <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;sentence&gt;</span> is the sentence prefix.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="103.52" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 103.52" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,103.52) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 97.61 C 0 100.87 2.64 103.52 5.91 103.52 L 647.24 103.52 C 650.51 103.52 653.15 100.87 653.15 97.61 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 79.41 L 651.18 79.41 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 88)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Prompt template 4</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 14.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.07em;--ltx-fo-height:3.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.04em;font-size:10pt;" height="53.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 53.28)" width="609.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.07em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;USER&gt; Is the pronoun "it" in the following sentence ambiguous? Provide the answer and explain. 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: &lt;sentence&gt;<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt;<span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">See the excerpt below for an example prompt
and the LLM’s response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p">&lt;USER&gt; Is the pronoun ’it’ in the following sentence ambiguous? 
<br class="ltx_break">Provide the answer and explain. 
<br class="ltx_break">Sentence: The railway worker attached the engine to the goods wagon and sent it __.</p>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_p">&lt;ASSISTANT&gt; Yes, the pronoun ’it’ in the given sentence is ambiguous. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_p">The sentence "The railway worker attached the engine to the goods wagon and sent it __" does not clearly specify whether ’it’ refers to the ’engine’ or the ’goods wagon’. Without additional context, there are two possible interpretations:</p>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_p">1. The railway worker attached the engine to the goods wagon and then sent (moved) the engine.
2. The railway worker attached the engine to the goods wagon and then sent (moved) the goods wagon.</p>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_p">The ambiguity arises because ’it’ could be referring to either of the two nouns mentioned previously in the sentence without any clear indicators such as an article or modifying phrase. To remove this ambiguity, more context or specific language would be needed to clarify what exactly was sent.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs responses are divided into: (1) <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Unambiguous</span>, where the LLMs do not recognize the ambiguity of the pronoun, (2) <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ambiguous</span>, where the LLMs think that the two constituents are both possible to the referent, and (3) <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ambiguous + Mereo.</span>, where they also mention that the mereological object is also likely to be a referent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
